## Insert at Head

```javascript
function insertAtHead(head, val) {
  const newNode = { val, next: head };
  return newNode; // new head
}
```

## Delete a Node (Given Previous)

```javascript
prev.next = prev.next.next; // skip the node to delete
```

## Reverse a Linked List

Requires three pointers: `prev`, `curr`, and `next`. Save `next` before overwriting `curr.next`, then redirect `curr.next` to `prev`, then advance all three.

```javascript
function reverse(head) {
  let prev = null, curr = head;
  while (curr !== null) {
    const next = curr.next;  // SAVE next before overwriting
    curr.next = prev;        // redirect pointer
    prev = curr;             // advance prev
    curr = next;             // advance curr
  }
  return prev; // new head
}
```

The three-pointer pattern is the foundation for all in-place linked list modification. The critical rule: **save `curr.next` to a variable BEFORE modifying `curr.next`**. If you overwrite `curr.next` first, you permanently lose access to the rest of the list.

Fast/slow pointers traverse a linked list at different speeds to detect structural properties.

## Cycle Detection (Floyd's Algorithm)

```javascript
function hasCycle(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;
  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
    if (slow === fast) return true;
  }
  return false;
}
```

If a cycle exists, fast will eventually 'lap' slow inside the cycle and they meet. If no cycle, fast reaches `null` first.

## Finding the Middle

When `fast` reaches the end, `slow` is at the middle. For even-length lists, slow lands on the second of the two middle nodes.

```javascript
function findMiddle(head) {
  let slow = head, fast = head;
  while (fast && fast.next) {
    slow = slow.next;
    fast = fast.next.next;
  }
  return slow;
}
```

## Kth Node from End

Advance `fast` exactly `k` steps. Then move both at the same speed until `fast` reaches the last node. `slow` is now at the (n-k)th node - one step before the target, useful for deletion.

```javascript
let fast = head;
for (let i = 0; i < k; i++) fast = fast.next;
let slow = head;
while (fast.next) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next; }
// slow is now at the node BEFORE the kth from end
```

The **dummy head** (sentinel node) is a fake node inserted before the real head. Its sole purpose: eliminate special cases for operations on the list head.

Without a dummy: 'remove the nth node from end' has a special case when `n === length` (removing the head). With a dummy:

```javascript
function removeNthFromEnd(head, n) {
  const dummy = { val: 0, next: head };
  let fast = dummy, slow = dummy;
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) fast = fast.next; // advance n+1 steps
  while (fast) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next; }
  slow.next = slow.next.next; // remove the target
  return dummy.next; // real head (may have changed if original head was removed)
}
```

The dummy makes `slow` start one node before the target, so `slow.next = slow.next.next` correctly removes the target in all cases - including when the target is the original head.

Use a dummy head whenever: merging two lists, removing nodes, or any operation where the head might change or require special handling.

Many linked list problems have elegant recursive solutions. Merge two sorted lists recursively:

```javascript
function mergeSorted(l1, l2) {
  if (!l1) return l2;
  if (!l2) return l1;
  if (l1.val <= l2.val) {
    l1.next = mergeSorted(l1.next, l2);
    return l1;
  } else {
    l2.next = mergeSorted(l2.val, l1);
    return l2;
  }
}
```

The recursive merge is clean and easy to reason about. However, it uses O(n) stack space because the recursion depth equals the list length. For a list of 10,000 nodes in JavaScript, this can stack overflow (JS limit ~10,000–15,000 frames).

The iterative version uses O(1) space - a dummy head and two pointers. For production code or large inputs, prefer iterative. In interviews, either is acceptable unless the problem constraints suggest a very long list.

